If two clients share signals (sister organisations, shared codebase, etc.), document
the shared patterns here and the rule for splitting.

### <Parent group name>

<Client A> and <Client B> are both part of <Parent>. Some artefacts span both:

- **Recurring `<Meeting Name>`** → bills to <project> (the convention you actually use)
- **Codebase `<repo>`** → serves both clients; pick by ticket / commit context

---

## How I bill (description style + project selection)

Your own conventions. The skill applies these when proposing entries.

**Description style — client-readable, 5–15 words, outcome-focused.** Most
descriptions get sent to clients with the invoice. Lead with the *result*, not the
tools or files. Don't name specific code files, internal app names, or chat partners
unless essential.

Real examples from your entries (paste 3–5 here once you have them):
- "<example outcome description>"
- "<example outcome description>"
- "<example outcome description>"

**Bill by ticket / work item, not by window or app.** One sustained block of work
on one ticket = one Harvest entry, even when the surface switched between VS Code,
Power Apps, Teams, browser. Don't fragment by window-title change.

**Ticket-catalog caveat.** If you maintain a dump of all active tickets, remember
that catalog existence ≠ today's billing target. A ticket may exist with a
thematically-matching name but the day's work bills somewhere else.

**Codebase path is a codebase signal, not a billing signal.** A repo can serve
multiple Harvest projects. Use the work-item context to pick the project.

---

## Personal browsing patterns to exclude (do not bill)

List anything that's *clearly* personal so the skill drops it from time blocks
automatically. Be specific — generic categories invite false positives.

- Flight / travel searches (Google Flights, destination image searches, travel guides)
- Social media (personal scrolling on FB / IG / X / LinkedIn)
- News, weather, shopping, personal email
- Pomodoro timer pages (timer overlay only — not work itself)
- Home admin pages (router admin, smart home, NAS, etc.)

---

## Notes on inference

Any standing rules that don't fit the categories above. Examples:

- **Edge profile in title is a strong client signal.** Pattern: `... - <ProfileName> - <YourName> - Microsoft Edge`. Treat profile name as the client unless this file overrides.
- **Teams meeting names** are reliable block boundaries.
- **ActivityWatch noise**: the Power Platform admin center page polls in the background and produces lots of 0-duration events. Ignore short blips.
- **Harvest itself** (Timesheet tab in browser) → internal admin, not billable client work.
- **Pure `claude.exe` time** accompanying timesheet-automation files (`Claude/Scheduled/`, `Pictures/WorkScreenshots/`, `Timesheets/`) → internal admin. `claude.exe` adjacent to a billable client's app stack should be attributed to that client.
